> Anyhow, my understanding is that currently calling `focus()`will scroll to the element.

Oh, sorry for misunderstanding this; I was assuming it did not, without testing.

> I was under this impression that `.focus()` is the same as `.focus(undefined)` and is different from `.focus({scrollOptions: undefined})`. Am I wrong?

Yeah, that's not correct. In Web IDL, supplying undefined for a dictionary is the same as supplying a dictionary with all its values set to its defaults. (And `undefined` is how to trigger defaults for dictionary members.)

> If that is true so `focus()` with no parameter should scroll (and if this is the same as `.focus({scrollOptions: undefined})`) what is the best option for indicating no scrolling in FocusOptions without adding the `none` to the `ScrollBehavior`?

I think adding to ScrollBehavior is the best option, as it's much more clear for developers. If you're not willing to do that... perhaps we should take a step back and think about what API we want to present to developers, before getting into the IDL details.

For example, one possibility I just came up with is:

```js
el.focus({ scroll: "auto" });
el.focus(); // same as above
el.focus({ scroll: "none" });
el.focus({ scroll: "instant" });
el.focus({ scroll: "smooth" });
el.focus({ scroll: "smooth", scrollOptions: { block: "start", inline: "end" });
```

Getting this properly integrated with the various underlying specs is difficult, but I'd be happy to help there. But first I think we should work on agreement on what API we want, regardless of how it integrates with other specs.

I just had a discussion with @bokand regarding this as well. To avoid those two pitfalls how about adding the extra field in `FocusOptions` but instead of having it as enum (like in your suggestion) have it as just a boolean to indicate whether to call into `scrollIntoView` or not as the behavior of the scroll already exists in `ScrollIntoViewOptions`.

```
FocusOptions {
   ScrollIntoViewOptions scrollOptions;
   bool scrollEnabled;
}

```
So if one calls `.focus({scrollEnabled:false})` we don't scroll and we ignore the scrollOptions if it is there. If `scrollEnabled` is true we pass the `scrollOptions` as is to `scrollIntoView`.

From what I understand,
referring the dictionary in CSSOM like below:
```
enum ScrollLogicalPosition { "start", "center", "end", "nearest" };
enum ScrollBehavior { "auto", "instant", "smooth" };

dictionary ScrollOptions {
    ScrollBehavior behavior = "auto";
};

dictionary ScrollIntoViewOptions : ScrollOptions {
  ScrollLogicalPosition block = "center";
  ScrollLogicalPosition inline = "center";
};

```

the version of @domenic is

```
void focus(optional FocusOptions options);

enum ScrollBehavior { "auto", "none",  "instant", "smooth" }; // Add "none" to the current CSSOM spec
dictionary FocusOptions {
  ScrollIntoViewOptions scrollOptions;
};

```

and the version of @NavidZ is
```
void focus();
void focus(optional FocusOptions options);

// Use the ScrollBehavior as defined in the current CSSOM spec
dictionary FocusOptions {
  ScrollIntoViewOptions scrollOptions;
  boolean scrollEnabled;
};
```
Am I understanding right?

> That was discussed up at #2787 (comment) and rejected as that prevents future expansion to other non-scroll-related options.

"future expansion to other non-scroll-related options" is about adding "none" to the [ScrollBehavior](https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom-view/#enumdef-scrollbehavior)?

> I didn't quite get this statement? What is wrong with ScrolIntoViewOptions? It has all attributes related to doing the scroll action and nothing else.

As we're finding out in this thread, it's designed in the context of a very specific API (scrollIntoView), which has legacy constraints (an existing boolean signature), and is assumed to always scroll. Both of these make using it for other APIs, such as .focus(), quite awkward. I don't think it's the right tool for the job. Instead, we should endeavor to use the underlying concepts and algorithms, but not the exact IDL constructs.

> I assume you are saying that we should add "none" to ScrollBehavior enum. Right?

No. My proposal is the one at https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/2787#issuecomment-315940175.

If it helps, here is the IDL for my proposal, since it keeps being forgotten and misinterpreted:

```webidl
enum FocusScrollBehavior { "none", "auto", "instant", "smooth" };

dictionary FocusScrollOptions {
  ScrollLogicalPosition block = "center";
  ScrollLogicalPosition inline = "center";
};

dictionary FocusOptions {
  FocusScrollBehavior scroll = "auto";
  FocusScrollOptions scrollOptions;
}

void focus(optional FocusOptions options);
```

The only objections I've seen to this so far have been on code-structuring grounds (i.e. unease with duplication). But none of them have been on usability grounds. The duplication is relatively easy to fix, with no web-visible changes, via changes to Web IDL (e.g. dictionary mixins; enum inheritance). But an API with poor usability is not easy to fix, and has bad consequences for our users.

Thanks to the opinion from @fantasai and discussion with @anawhj, I came up with the IDL like this:

```javascript
void focus(optional FocusScrollOptions options);

dictionary FocusScrollOptions : ScrollIntoViewOptions {
  boolean preventScroll = false;
};
```
with referring CSSOM spec: 
```javascript
enum ScrollBehavior { "auto", "instant", "smooth" };
dictionary ScrollOptions {
    ScrollBehavior behavior = "auto";
};
dictionary ScrollIntoViewOptions : ScrollOptions {
  ScrollLogicalPosition block = "center";
  ScrollLogicalPosition inline = "center";
};
```
- Use [ScrollBehavior](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#enumdef-scrollbehavior) without adding "none" value to avoid contradicting instructions
- Define a new dictionary "FocusScrollOptions" to make it clear to use the API
- [ScrollIntoViewOptions](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#dictdef-scrollintoviewoptions) is ignored if preventScroll is true

I agree with @NavidZ about adding "none" to ScrollBehavior isn't proper.
ScrollBehavior is also used in not only scrollIntoView() but also scrollTo(), scrollBy() and giving "none" to  
 [behavior](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#dom-scrolloptions-behavior) parameter is meaningless for those APIs.

But I define a new dictionary "FocusScrollOptions", because I think it's simpler than
 `focus({ preventScroll, scrollOptions })`.
Also it's more clear than  `focus((boolean or object) arg)` when we design similar to [scrollIntoView()](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#dom-element-scrollintoview).
It is confusing what that `false` means when we read  `el.focus(false)`.

SO, it could be used like:

```js
// Default focus-based scrolling
el.focus();
el.focus({ preventScroll: false }); // same as above

// Disable focus-based scrolling
el.focus({ preventScroll: true} );

// Customize focus-based scrolling
el.focus({ behavior: "smooth" });
el.focus({ block: "nearest", behavior: "smooth" });

```
@domenic @NavidZ @bokand how do you think?

I agree with @jihyerish's opinions. Adding none into `ScrollBehavior` would be risky because `scrollTo()` or `scrollIntoView()` can also use the `ScrollBehavior` enum as one of the method's parameters. In addition, the `focus()` behavior that makes no scrolling would be a very exceptional case to support few use cases, so the preventScroll with boolean type as an first parameter of `focus()` makes me unclear.

In the discussion with @jihyerish, we can hit on an additional idea about a way to simply handle no scrolling. If we don't need to consider the option for no scrolling in the parameter of focus(), the solution seems very simple and obvious as follows. It only makes minimal changes in WHATWG and no changes in CSSOM.
```javascript
void focus(optional ScrollIntoViewOptions options);
```
To support `focus()` behavior that makes no scrolling, we can define a new API(method) named `preventScroll()`. In several event handlers, `e.preventScroll()` makes no scrolling even though the event causes a scroll offset's change. We can write codes using the new API as follows.
```javascript
el.focus({preventScroll: true});
// same as above
el.onfocus = function(e) {
  e.preventScroll();
}
```

It could be used in many other use cases as well. As a focus-related issue, @RByers mentioned a use case in [#834](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/834#issuecomment-317757573) last week regarding how to set no scrolling when a focused box is out of viewport and a key input happens in the box. We can write codes to support the use case using `e.preventScroll()` as follows.
```javascript
// el is the focused input box
el.addEventListener("keypress", function() {
  e.preventScroll();
}
```
In addition to above cases, there are some use cases using `e.preventScroll()` and it would be only solution to support some use cases like preventing scrolling in scrollable container (`onscroll`). If the new API could be reasonable, I can list the related use cases and discuss with the parameter structure of `focus()` as well.

> That doesn't seem to work because it makes things very confusing if we add non-scroll related options to focus. E.g., let's consider if we later added speak for some kind of audio announcement of scrolling. Then the code.

Ah, I missed something that you pointed out. Then the IDL would be:

```js
void focus(optional FocusOptions options);

dictionary FocusOptions {
  boolean preventScroll = false;
  ScrollIntoViewOptions scrollOptions;  
};
```

right? I also changed the naming of option for considering future expansion to other non-scroll-related options.
Then it could be used as:
```js
// Disable focus-based scrolling
el.focus({ preventScroll: true });

// Customize focus-based scrolling
el.focus({ preventScroll: false, scrollOptions: { behavior: "smooth" } });
el.focus({ scrollOptions: { behavior: "smooth" } }); // same as above

el.focus({ scrollOptions: { block: "nearest", behavior: "smooth" }});
```

@domenic @NavidZ Thanks for your reviews! :+1:  
As @NavidZ pointed out, I removed the step about checking if the element is in a scrollable region.

@zcorpan could you review this PR whether it aligns well with CSSOM Spec?

See https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/pull/1805 for a proposal to move forward here. My idea with the new dictionary is that it should reflect the behavior of `focus()`, such that the following are equivalent:
```
elm.focus();
elm.focus({ scrollOptions: {} });
```
If this approach sounds good, the change necessary in this PR is to change from `ScrollIntoViewOptions` to `FocusScrollOptions`.

Per [w3c/csswg-drafts#1805 (comment)](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/pull/1805#issuecomment-337801758), it seems to be resolved as only applying `preventDefault` into focus() on CSSOM-View side. I think the following two options could  be considered:

```javascript
// option 1
dictionary FocusOptions {
  boolean preventScroll = false;
};
void focus(optional FocusOptions options);

// option 2
void focus(optional boolean preventScroll);
```

As discussed above, I would prefer to have option 1 for the future extension, but I'm not sure it's right. If we decide one of the options and update the changes, I believe we could finalize this long journey where we have worked since last June as a first phase for adding `scrollOptions` into focus().

It means you prefer 'option 2' directly using a boolean type value as the parameter of focus(), right? If right, it seems difficult to understand the meaning of `false`, so I suggested 'option 1' to show the functionality of `preventScroll`. I'm wondering your concern about 'the easier sell'.

```javascript
// option 2
void focus(optional boolean preventScroll);

// example of option 2
focus(false); // same as focus();
```

Meanwhile, I tried to extract the relevant descriptions from the [link](https://extensiblewebmanifesto.org/) @zcorpan mentioned. In particular, I totally agree on `#1` below, while I believe this thread was stemmed from the motivation. Without `preventScroll`, there is no way to handle the functionality in libraries.

1. To enable libraries to do more, browser vendors should provide new low-level capabilities that expose the possibilities of the underlying platform as closely as possible.

2. Focus on adding new low-level capabilities to the web platform that are secure and efficient.

3. Reduce the rate of growth in complexity, and therefore bugs, in implementations.

4. Require less developer education for new features. Educational materials can build off of concepts that are already in the platform.

I pushed new commits to make focus() have only `preventScroll` as an option depending on above comments.

The IDL is:
```js
dictionary FocusOptions {
  boolean preventScroll = false;
};
void focus(optional FocusOptions options);
```
If `preventScroll` is true, then the scroll triggered by focus is disabled.
Otherwise the element will be scrolled into view with UA-defined manners.

